# Lovecruz — Complete VPS Deployment Guide

Everything from first login to a working HTTPS production setup, in the order you actually run it. Replace anything in `<...>` with your real values. Run everything as `root` (or with `sudo`) from Windows Command Prompt using `ssh`.

---

## PHASE 0 — Before you touch the terminal

1. Buy the Hostinger VPS.
2. Note the VPS **public IP address** (shown in the Hostinger panel).
3. In **Hostinger → Domain → DNS Records**, point these A records to that IP:

| Type | Name  | Points to        |
|------|-------|------------------|
| A    | @     | `<YOUR_VPS_IP>`  |
| A    | api   | `<YOUR_VPS_IP>`  |
| A    | media | `<YOUR_VPS_IP>`  |
| A    | radio | `<YOUR_VPS_IP>`  |
| A    | turn  | `<YOUR_VPS_IP>`  |
| A    | ws    | `<YOUR_VPS_IP>`  |

DNS can take anywhere from a few minutes to a few hours to propagate. You can start the server setup immediately — you don't need to wait for DNS before Phase 1.

---

## PHASE 1 — Connect to the VPS

From Windows Command Prompt:

```
ssh root@<YOUR_VPS_IP>
```

Example (do not use literally):

```
ssh root@185.xxx.xxx.xxx
```

- Type `yes` the first time you connect.
- Enter your VPS root password when prompted.

Everything from here on runs **inside the SSH session** (i.e., on the VPS), not in Command Prompt directly.

---

## PHASE 2 — Base system setup

### Step 1. Update Ubuntu
```
apt update && apt upgrade -y
```

### Step 2. Install required base packages
```
apt install -y git curl wget unzip build-essential software-properties-common
```

### Step 3. Install Node.js 24
```
curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_24.x | bash -

This adds the official NodeSource repository for Node.js 24 to your Ubuntu system.
Wait until it finishes and returns you to the prompt, for example:

Then run the second command:
apt install -y nodejs
```

Verify:
```
node -v
npm -v
```

### Step 4. Install PM2 (process manager)
```
npm install -g pm2
```

Verify:
```
pm2 -v
```

### Step 5. Install Nginx
```
apt install nginx -y
systemctl enable nginx
systemctl start nginx
```

### Step 6. Install PostgreSQL
```
apt install postgresql postgresql-contrib -y
systemctl enable postgresql
systemctl start postgresql
```

### Step 7. Install Redis
```
apt install redis-server -y
systemctl enable redis-server
systemctl start redis-server
```

### Step 8. Install Coturn (TURN server — don't configure yet)
```
apt install coturn -y
```

### Step 9. Install FFmpeg
```
apt install ffmpeg -y
```

### Step 10. Confirm Git is installed
```
git --version
```

---

## PHASE 3 — Deploy your backend

### Step 11. Clone your repository
```
cd /home
git clone <YOUR_GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL>
```

Exa]mple:
```
git clone https://github.com/<YOUR_USERNAME>/<YOUR_REPOSITORY>.git
```

### Step 12. Enter the backend folder
```
cd <YOUR_REPOSITORY>/backend
```

### Step 13. Install npm packages
```
npm install
```

### Step 14. Create your `.env` file
```
nano .env
```

Paste your environment variables, replacing placeholders:
```
MEDIASOUP_ANNOUNCED_IP=<YOUR_VPS_PUBLIC_IP>
DATABASE_URL=<YOUR_DATABASE_URL>
REDIS_URL=<YOUR_REDIS_URL>
TURN_PASSWORD=<YOUR_TURN_PASSWORD>
```

Save and exit nano:
```
Ctrl + O   (write)
Enter
Ctrl + X   (exit)
```

### Step 15. Run database migrations
```
npm run migrate
```
or, depending on your project structure:
```
node scripts/migrate.js
```

### Step 16. Start the backend with PM2
```
pm2 start server.js --name lovecruz-api
```
or:
```
pm2 start src/server.js --name lovecruz-api
```
(use whichever entry file matches your project)

### Step 17. Persist PM2 across reboots
```
pm2 save
pm2 startup
```
`pm2 startup` will print a second command — copy that exact line, paste it, and run it. Then run:
```
pm2 save
```
again.

### Step 18. Verify the backend is running
```
pm2 status
```
or
```
pm2 logs
```
You should see `lovecruz-api` with status `online`.

---

## PHASE 4 — Nginx reverse proxy (now that the app is actually running)

### Step 19. Create the Nginx site config
```
nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/lovecruz
```

Paste the following, adjusting domains/ports to match your actual services:

```nginx
server {
    server_name api.lovecruz.fun;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;
    }
}

server {
    server_name media.lovecruz.fun;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:4000;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }
}

server {
    server_name radio.lovecruz.fun;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8000;
    }
}
```

Save and exit (`Ctrl + O`, `Enter`, `Ctrl + X`).

This tells Nginx:
- `api.lovecruz.fun` → Node API on port 3000
- `media.lovecruz.fun` → Mediasoup/media service on port 4000
- `radio.lovecruz.fun` → radio service on port 8000

### Step 20. Enable the site
```
ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/lovecruz /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
```

### Step 21. Test the config
```
nginx -t
```

### Step 22. Restart Nginx if the test passed
```
systemctl restart nginx
```

At this point, confirm DNS has actually propagated:
```
ping api.lovecruz.fun
```
(Ctrl+C to stop). It should resolve to your VPS IP. If it doesn't yet, wait before moving to SSL.

---

## PHASE 5 — SSL with Let's Encrypt

### Step 23. Install Certbot
```
apt install certbot python3-certbot-nginx -y
```

### Step 24. Request certificates
```
certbot --nginx
```

Certbot will detect your Nginx config and prompt you to choose which domains to secure, e.g.:
```
lovecruz.fun
api.lovecruz.fun
media.lovecruz.fun
radio.lovecruz.fun
```

It will automatically obtain certificates, configure HTTPS, offer to redirect HTTP → HTTPS, and set up auto-renewal.

### Step 25. Verify auto-renewal works
```
certbot renew --dry-run
```

---

## PHASE 6 — Firewall (UFW)

### Step 26. Allow required ports
```
ufw allow OpenSSH
ufw allow 80
ufw allow 443
ufw allow 3478
ufw allow 5349
ufw allow 3000
ufw allow 4000
```

### Step 27. Open the Mediasoup UDP port range
```
ufw allow 40000:49999/udp
```

### Step 28. Enable the firewall
```
ufw enable
```

Confirm with:
```
ufw status
```

---

## PHASE 7 — Final checks

### Step 29. Confirm backend is alive
```
pm2 status
curl http://localhost:3000
```

### Step 30. Confirm Nginx is proxying correctly
```
curl -I https://api.lovecruz.fun
```

### Step 31. Confirm Postgres/Redis are running
```
systemctl status postgresql
systemctl status redis-server
```

### Step 32. Test from a browser
Visit:
- `https://lovecruz.fun`
- `https://api.lovecruz.fun`
- `https://media.lovecruz.fun`
- `https://radio.lovecruz.fun`

All should load over HTTPS with a valid certificate (padlock icon, no warnings).

---

## What's intentionally NOT included here

Two pieces depend entirely on your specific project code/config, so they can't be generic copy-paste commands:

- **Coturn configuration** (`/etc/turnserver.conf`) — needs your actual TURN realm, external IP, and credentials.
- **Mediasoup worker/router config and the radio (RTMP/HLS) service setup** — these live inside your application code, not the OS.

If you share your `turnserver.conf` requirements or your Mediasoup/radio service entry points, I can write those configs out fully in the same step-by-step style.